## Quick Reads
- [Resilient Omnichannel Retail Connectivity](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/resilient-omnichannel-retail-connectivity/): The store is a fulfillment node now, and the network has to keep up. This solution brief covers what device-saturated stores, aging MPLS and store-led fulfillment demand of retail connectivity, and how GTT&#039;s managed SD-WAN, flexible access and global Tier 1 backbone keep every site trading with sub-second failover and new locations online in days. Kiabi, HMY and Greenyard results included.
- [Simplified Global Retail Operations](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/simplified-global-retail-operations/): Many global retailers juggle 10 to 15 providers, each with its own console, contract and support line. This solution brief shows how GTT consolidates networking, security, cloud and voice onto one platform and one bill, with one GTT EnvisionEDGE device per store, a global voice service and a co-managed model that hands Day-2 work to GTT. Greenyard and HMY put figures against the result.
- [Right-Cloud Retail](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/right-cloud-retail/): Getting AI to work in the store is a network problem before it is a math problem. This solution brief explains why hybrid cloud has become retail&#039;s default operating model and how GTT Managed Hybrid Cloud runs every workload where it performs best, at the store edge, in private cloud or in managed public cloud, on GTT&#039;s own global Tier 1 backbone with predictable pricing. Distrelec and Kiabi results included.
- [Beyond Just SASE: A Pragmatic Blueprint for Secure, Efficient Connectivity to Protect All Users at the Edge](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/beyond-just-sase/): Manufacturing is now the most-attacked industry in the world. When security is layered on top of a fragmented network, one breach can shut down global production. The technology isn&#039;t the problem. It&#039;s the security architecture the technology runs on.
- [Cloud-Smart Factory Innovation](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/cloud-smart-factory-innovation/): Cloud-first has given way to cloud-smart. Manufacturers are rebalancing workloads across private, multi-tenant and public cloud based on actual performance, not architectural convention. The problem isn&#039;t cloud adoption. It&#039;s the absence of an infrastructure designed to make cloud work for manufacturing operations.
- [Zero Trust, Everywhere](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/zero-trust-everywhere/): The manufacturing workforce now operates from everywhere, plant floors, home offices, contractor networks, OEM partner systems. The perimeter that access controls were designed to protect no longer exists. When a VPN trusts the network, every compromised credential becomes a global blast radius.
- [Expert-Led IT Transformation](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/expert-led-it-transformation/): Manufacturing transformation programs don&#039;t fail on technology. They fail on execution. Network modernization, cloud migration, OT/IT security, compliance with NIS2, these are running in parallel, across dozens of countries, on teams that were not scaled to absorb them.
- [Built-In Cyber Resilience](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/built-in-cyber-resilience/): Manufacturing is now the most-attacked industry in the world. When security is layered on top of a fragmented network, one breach can shut down global production. The technology isn&#039;t the problem. It&#039;s the security architecture the technology runs on.
- [Seamless Multi-Site Connectivity](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/seamless-multi-site-connectivity/): Smart manufacturing runs on data that moves between plants, suppliers and cloud environments in real time. When the network can&#039;t deliver consistent performance across every site, modernization stalls. Most manufacturing networks weren&#039;t built for this. They were stitched together market by market, and that&#039;s where the problem starts.
- [How Managed Network Services Make A Critical Difference For Operations](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/how-managed-network-services-make-a-critical-difference-for-operations/): Manufacturing is a high intensity business. Maintaining profitability while managing the supplychain demands of delivering on-time and on-budget is a priority, as is managing the inventories ofraw materials to meet the needs of a busy plant.
- [Manufacturing: Why Technology Partners Matter In Uncertain Times](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/why-technology-partners-matter-in-uncertain-times/): Manufacturers globally have faced serious challenges in recent years. Technology skills gaps, supply chain disruption and the increasing need for automation, along with the secure networks required to support manufacturing advancement, have manufacturers turning to technology; and to external expert partners, to meet enterprise goals.
- [Get The Lowdown On The Smart Factory &#038; The Next Wave of Innovation](https://www.gtt.net/resources/quick-reads/get-the-lowdown-on-the-smart-factory-the-next-wave-of-innovation/): Manufacturers understand that they must stay up to date with the latest developments in technology to be competitive. At the same time, technology is moving fast, with the AI revolution pushing the boundaries of possibility in many sectors and industry.
